name: Logging
order: 201
------------

Logging is an invaluable feature of any complex software system.
It is the simplest way to get introspection into a running program.
Code leaves a text message trail that can be searched, filtered, and examined for troubleshooting.

_Collider.JAM_ provides custom logging functions _log()_ and _dir()_ available in the global context. They are similar to the regular browser _console.log()_ and _console.dir()_, but allow certain customizations, e.g. when we want to redirect messages to other targets, maybe a custom debug console. Also, the standard log() implementation prints out ">" prefix to separate regular logging messages from the system messages printed out by Collider.JAM itself and messages from different log levels.

The usage is the same, as methods of _console_, to print a message to the browser console:

    log('debug message')

To dump the object structure:

    dir( player )

You still can use regular _console.log()_ and _console.dir()_ with similar effect, but the above functions are more compact and allow more flexibility.

The log function also contains a list of subfunctions to specify intent more precisely:

    log.err('error message prefixed with "!"')
    log.warn('warning message prefixed with "?"')
    log.out('default message - the same as log(), prefixed with ">"')
    log.debug('debug message prefixed with "."')
    log.trace('trace message prefixed with ":"')

Each type of log message will be marked with a unique prefix. If you want to drop prefixes and get behavior identical to console.log, just use the raw() function:

    log.raw('message without prefixes')

You can also dump an object:

    // dump player object structure and values
    log.dump( player )

This is different from a plain _dir()_, since not only dumps the object structure, but prints out property values.

Implement the player.dump() function returning a string if you want to customize the dump of a player.

See [/log](#./log) for the full list of logging subfunctions.



== Beware of Log Overflows

The _Collider.JAM_ game loop runs 60 or more cycles per second (144 if run on a 144Hz screen). It is easy to overflood logs when logging each tick from multiple objects in the _/lab_.

The browser dev tools have a filter that can help to pinpoint needed messages, but even with this feature you can overflow the browser with log messages to the point it becomes unresponsive.

Sometimes, you'll have to kill the tab to stop the particularly heavy log stream.












